3.11.2.m:
You, Agni, are Rudra, the asura of high heaven;
you rule as the troop of the Maruts, the rich feast;
you travel along with the ruddy winds, bringing blessing to the home;
you as Pūṣan protect the worshipers now yourself.
Gods, rest upon the gods!
First ones, rest upon the second ones!
Second ones, rest upon the third ones!
Third ones, rest upon the fourth ones!
Fourth ones, run to the fifth ones!
Fifth ones, rest upon the sixth ones!

3.11.6.m:
sacrifice to the gods for him who is devoted to the gods.
For Agni gives a courser to the clan,
he who turns towards all peoples,
Agni, who is well-abiding for wealth;
he, being pleased, goes to what is choice;
bring refreshment to the praisers.
Questioned in heaven, questioned on earth,
Agni, questioned, entered all herbs;
Agni Vaiśvānara, questioned with might—
may he protect us by day, may he [protect us] from injury by night.

3.11.8.m:
He conquers re-death,
who piles the Nāciketa fire,
and who knows it thus.
Prajāpati, desiring offspring, performed ascetic heat.
He cast forth gold.
He cast it into the fire;
it did not please him.
He cast it a second time;
it did not please him at all.
He cast it a third time;

it did not please him at all.
He cast it into the Vaiśvānara fire within his own heart;
it pleased him.
Therefore gold is the smallest of riches,
[yet] the dearest when enjoyed,
for it was born from the heart.
Indeed, he found no one
to whom he could lead that sacrificial gift;
he led it to his own right hand;
he received it.

3.12.1.m:
"To you, O most Angiras-like..."; "May we attain that desire, O Agni...";
"To you of the quarters..."; "All the quarters...";
"May Anumati approve of us today..."; "Do you, O Anumati, approve...";
"Desire of what has come to be..."; "Desire in the beginning...";
"The brahman born first..."; "Father of the Virājs...";
"The sacrifice of wealth..."; "This sacrifice...";
"Auspicious waters..."; "Then indeed I see...";
"To you they bring, who the mud-built [ramparts]...";
"The gods formerly by what is later..."; "Exhalation and inhalation...";
"The bearer of oblations..."; "Well-sacrificed..."

3.12.2.m:
These indeed are the seven doors of the heavenly realm,
named the Falcons of Heaven, the Findings.
Hope guards the first;
Desire the second;
brahman the third;
Sacrifice the fourth;
Waters the fifth;
Agni Possessed-of-Tribute the sixth;
Finding the seventh.
He indeed finds the heavenly realm;
his movement is at will in the heavenly realm,
who sacrifices with these offerings,
and who knows them thus.
Upon these offerings,
he should give a four-year-old cow as choice portion, and a brass vessel,
and an ornament for a woman, for prosperity.

3.12.3.m:
Through ascetic heat the gods attained divinity in the beginning;
through ascetic heat the seers found the sun.
Through ascetic heat let us drive away rivals and foes,
by which this all, whatever exists, is encompassed.
Let us worship the first-born god with an oblation,
the self-existent brahman, which is the highest ascetic heat.
He indeed is the son, he the father, he the mother;
as ascetic heat indeed did the first wonder come into being.
Through faith a god attains divinity;
faith is the foundation, the divine queen of the world.

so that we may revel together with the gods.
Whose foundation is the broad midspace,
from whom the gods and all beings were born,
that Truth, shining, has come to our sacrifice,
brahman rejoicing at the offerings.
Under the control of Mind this all has come to be;
Mind never goes under the control of another.
For formidable is the god, mightier than might;
may he, being pleased, come to our sacrifice.
The overlord of intentions and thoughts,

3.12.4.m:
The heavenly realm became hidden from the gods.
They said to Prajāpati:
'Prajāpati, the heavenly realm has become hidden from us;
seek it.'
He sought it with the sacrificial rites;
he did not find it with the sacrificial rites.
He sought it with the offerings;
he found it with the offerings.
That is the offering-nature of the offerings.
They are indeed called searchings;
them they call offerings cryptically,
for the gods are fond of the cryptic.

3.12.5.m:
Agni is the Hotṛ of the Pañcahotṛ formulas;
Speech is the Hotṛ of the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ formulas;
Mahāhavis is the Hotṛ of the Saptahotṛ formulas.
This indeed is the Caturhotṛ nature of the Caturhotṛ formulas,
and also the Pañcahotṛ nature.
To him indeed all quarters yield,
who knows thus.
This indeed is the all-knowledge;
this is the medicine;
this is the fivefold straight path leading to the heavenly realm.

With them he conquered disputation,
and all evil;
he went to heaven.
Whoever studies these
conquers disputation,
and all evil;
he goes to heaven.
With them, one desiring the heavenly realm should pile the fire altar;
with them, one desiring a complete lifespan,
or one desiring offspring and domestic animals.

In front he lays down the Daśahotṛ [bricks] facing north, according to the words,
and the heart, the two Yajuses, and the two wives;
to the south, the Caturhotṛ [bricks] facing east;
to the west, the Pañcahotṛ [bricks] facing north;
to the north, the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ [bricks] facing east;
above, the Saptahotṛ [bricks] facing east,
and the heart, the Yajuses, and the wives;
according to their spaces the Grahas,
according to their spaces the Pratigrahas and the Lokaṃpṛṇā bricks.
All these deities become pleased and propitiated for him;

he piles the fire altar accompanied by the gods.
It should be piled to the measure of a chariot;
the chariot is indeed a vajra;
by means of the vajra he lays low his evil rival.
It should be piled to the measure of the side wing [of a chariot];
as large as the side wing,
so large indeed is the chariot;
he piles it just to the measure of a chariot.
This world indeed he conquers by an animal sacrifice,
and by the Agniṣṭoma;

the midspace by the Ukthya;
heaven by the Atirātra;
all worlds by the Ahīna rite,
and by the sattra.
A boon is the dakṣiṇā;
by a boon he redeems a boon,
for oneself is a boon.
He gives twenty-one as the dakṣiṇā;
twenty-onefold indeed from here is the heavenly realm;
he attains the heavenly realm.

That sun is the twenty-first;
he attains that sun.
He gives a hundred;
man has a lifespan of a hundred years and a hundred faculties;
he is established in lifespan and faculties.
He gives a thousand;
the heavenly realm is commensurate with a thousand;
[it is] for the winning of the heavenly realm.
He gives the dakṣiṇā according to the bricks,
[representing] all ages of life,

for the attainment of everything,
for the securing of everything.
If he should not find [them],
he should give that many stirred drinks or boiled rice messes;
he attains that wish
for the sake of which wish the fire altar is piled.
Rather, he should give a pregnant four-year-old heifer,
for she is indeed all ages of life,
for the attainment of everything,
for the securing of everything.

He gives gold;
possessing the light of gold indeed, he goes to the heavenly realm.
He gives a garment;
thereby he lengthens his lifespan.
He should sacrifice on the third of the altar;
for the gods are triply true;
he piles the fire possessed of truth.
One should recite this Brāhmaṇa at the animal sacrifice,
not at other sacrifices.
Whosoever indeed knows the four Hotṛs to be satisfied at every pressing,

He is satisfied with offspring and domestic animals;
the drinking of soma bends down toward him.
These, truly, are the Caturhotṛs to be satisfied at every pressing:
those Brahmins who know much.
If he should not bring a sacrificial gift to them,
it would be an imperfect sacrifice;
they would strip away his fire.
He should give to them according to his faith;
thereby this [sacrifice] is made well offered;
they do not strip away his fire.

3.12.9.m:
The eastern is called the great quarter of the verses;
the southern they declare to be the boundless [quarter] of the sacrificial formulas;
the western belongs to the Atharvans and Angirases;
the northern is called the great quarter of the chants.
By verses the god travels in heaven in the forenoon;
he stands in the Yajurveda
at the middle of the day;
by the Sāmaveda he is magnified at sunset:
not devoid of the three Vedas, the sun moves.
All form, they say, is born of the verses;
all motion is indeed ever belonging to the sacrificial formula;

all fiery energy has indeed ever the nature of the chants;
all this indeed was created by brahman alone.
The Vaiśya class, they say, was born of the verses;
the Yajurveda they call the womb of the Kṣatriya;
the Sāmaveda is the origin of the Brahmins:
this word the ancients spoke to the ancients.
Piling the conspicuous fire,
the ancient all-creators, immortal,
consecrated for a hundred thousand years,
sat at the sacrificial session.

Austerity was the householder;
brahman itself became the Brahman priest;
truth indeed was their Hotṛ priest,
when the all-creators sat [at the sacrifice].
Immortality chanted the Udgītha for them
for a thousand full-years;
the past indeed was their Prastotṛ priest;
and the future responded as the Pratihartṛ.
Exhalation became the Adhvaryu priest,
as they sought to win all this:

inhalation, knowing the ritual course,
stood as the Pratiprasthātṛ at the sacrificial rite.
The seasonal [deities] were the Upagātṛ choristers;
the seasons became the Sadasyas;
and the half-months and the months
became the Camasādhvaryus.
The fiery energy of brahman recited as Śastṛ;
renown became the Acchāvāka priest;
truth-order was their Praśāstṛ priest,
when the all-creators sat [at the sacrifice].

Vigor brought up the king [Soma];
overcoming power was the Dhruvagopa;
splendorous might praised the pressing stones,
when the all-creators sat [at the sacrifice].
Reverence sacrificed the Potṛ's share;
brilliance sacrificed the Neṣṭṛ's share;
knowing truth from the Āgnīdhra hearth,
Faith herself indeed offered the sacrifice.
Food-refreshment was the wife of the all-creators;
intention pounded the oblation:

hunger and thirst, both formidable,
brought fuel to them.
Speech was their Subrahmaṇyā priestess,
knowing the meters' combinations,
weaving the ritual frameworks and day-closures on all sides
and the closures.
Day and night were the animal guardians;
the moments became the attendant-messengers;
Death then was the placer [of the victim];
the fierce lord of the clans was the slaughterer.
